var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"The API reference is generated from exported Arrhenius.jl docstrings. It includes constructors, solver functions, thermodynamic and transport methods, and diagnostics.\n\nFor usage paths, see reactors, flames, and the examples index.","title":"API"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#Arrhenius.R","page":"API","text":"const R = 8314.46261815324 #J/(kmol*K)\n\nGas constant\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.R"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#Arrhenius.one_atm","page":"API","text":"const one_atm = 101325.0 #pascal\n\nOne atm\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.one_atm"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.AbstractCoverageDependency","page":"API","text":"Supertype for composable coverage-dependency laws targeting species standard states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.AbstractCoverageDependency"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.BlowersMaselRate","page":"API","text":"BlowersMaselRate(A, b, Ea0, w)\n\nEnthalpy-dependent modified Arrhenius rate. Ea0 and the average bond energy w use J/kmol, temperature uses K, and the units of A follow reaction order. The intrinsic barrier must be nonnegative and w > Ea0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.BlowersMaselRate"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.BurnerFlame","page":"API","text":"A planar premixed flame with prescribed inlet mass flux.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.BurnerFlame"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.BurnerFlame-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution}","page":"API","text":"BurnerFlame(gas; mdot, T=300, P=one_atm, X, width=0.03, grid=nothing)\n\nConstruct a burner-stabilized premixed flame. mdot is the inlet mass flux in kg/(m² s). The energy equation is enabled unless set_temperature_profile! specifies a measured temperature profile. The default discretization=:finite_difference matches Cantera's original discretization. Use :conservative for finite-volume species and total enthalpy balances.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.BurnerFlame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.CatalyticImpingingJet","page":"API","text":"An axisymmetric jet impinging on an isothermal, chemically reacting surface.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.CatalyticImpingingJet"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.CatalyticImpingingJet-Tuple{Any, SurfaceMechanism}","page":"API","text":"CatalyticImpingingJet(gas, surface; reactants, mdot, T_inlet=300,\n                     T_surface=900, P=one_atm, width=.1, coverages)\n\nCouple an ideal gas jet to stationary surface coverages. The wall has fixed temperature and zero normal velocity. Surface gas production sets the wall species flux; surface reaction heat is removed by the prescribed thermostat. The gas must be the surface mechanism's companion phase, in its original order. Persistent bulk phases, deposition, and moving walls are not supported. Initialization uses a linear temperature profile and inlet gas composition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.CatalyticImpingingJet"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.CatalyticNetwork","page":"API","text":"CatalyticNetwork(network; surfaces)\n\nExtend a native reactor network with gas/surface species exchange. Reactors carrying surfaces must be isothermal; other network vessels retain their own energy settings. The state contains the network state followed by each surface's coverages and signed bulk deposition in kmol. No solid depletion or surface energy equation is assumed. catalytic_diagnostics reports combined gas, surface, and deposited-bulk mass and elemental inventories and their rates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.CatalyticNetwork"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.CounterflowDiffusionFlame","page":"API","text":"Axisymmetric opposed fuel/oxidizer jets with optional optically thin gas radiation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.CounterflowDiffusionFlame"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.CounterflowDiffusionFlame-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution}","page":"API","text":"CounterflowDiffusionFlame(gas; fuel, oxidizer, mdot_fuel, mdot_oxidizer,\n                         T_fuel=300, T_oxidizer=300, P=one_atm, width=.02)\n\nConstruct a native axisymmetric counterflow diffusion flame. Both inlet mass fluxes are positive magnitudes [kg/(m² s)], directed into the domain. The axial velocity is positive at the fuel inlet and negative at the oxidizer inlet. Initialization uses a native stoichiometric HP equilibrium and an error-function mixing layer. Supports mixture-averaged neutral ideal gas, with optional gray optically thin H2O/CO2 radiation (radiation=true).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.CounterflowDiffusionFlame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.CounterflowPremixedFlame","page":"API","text":"Premixed reactants opposed by a prescribed product stream.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.CounterflowPremixedFlame"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.CounterflowPremixedFlame-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution}","page":"API","text":"CounterflowPremixedFlame(gas; reactants, mdot_reactants, mdot_products,\n                        T_reactants=300, P=one_atm, width=.02)\n\nOpposed premixed reactants and products, with positive inlet mass-flux magnitudes in kg/(m² s). By default, the product temperature and composition are calculated by native HP equilibrium. Set both products and T_products for another prescribed product stream. No reference flame profiles are used to initialize.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.CounterflowPremixedFlame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.CounterflowTwinPremixedFlame","page":"API","text":"Half of a symmetric pair of premixed counterflow flames.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.CounterflowTwinPremixedFlame"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.CounterflowTwinPremixedFlame-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution}","page":"API","text":"CounterflowTwinPremixedFlame(gas; reactants, mdot, T=300, P=one_atm, width=.025)\n\nSolve one half of a symmetric pair of opposed premixed jets. width is the distance from the inlet to the symmetry plane. The plane has zero axial velocity, zero temperature and spread-rate gradients, and zero diffusive species fluxes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.CounterflowTwinPremixedFlame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.CoverageThermoModel","page":"API","text":"CoverageThermoModel(; species_names, thermo_type, thermo_coefficients,\n                    reference_coverage=1.0,\n                    dependencies=AbstractCoverageDependency[])\n\nCoverage-dependent surface standard-state thermodynamics. thermo_type uses the SurfaceMechanism convention (1 = NASA7, 2 = constant-cp) and thermo_coefficients uses its per-species row layout, so the base low-coverage states are evaluated with the existing _surface_species_thermo. reference_coverage is the standard-state reference coverage thetaref in (0, 1]; dependencies lists self- and cross-interaction objects whose target and influencing indices refer to `speciesnames`.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.CoverageThermoModel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.CoverageThermoModel-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"CoverageThermoModel(path::AbstractString)\n\nLoad SI model parameters prepared by mechanism/export_coverage_thermo.py. The parameter archive is JSON; Cantera is needed only for preprocessing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.CoverageThermoModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.CoverageThermoWorkspace","page":"API","text":"CoverageThermoWorkspace(model)\n\nReusable per-state output buffers. The arrays returned by coverage_thermo! alias this workspace, so full coverage sweeps evaluate without per-state allocation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.CoverageThermoWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.DustyGasParameters","page":"API","text":"Stationary porous-medium geometry; lengths are in m and permeability in m².\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.DustyGasParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.DustyGasTransport","page":"API","text":"DustyGasTransport(gas; porosity, mean_pore_radius, tortuosity=1,\n                  mean_particle_diameter=0, permeability=nothing,\n                  multicomponent_data=nothing)\n\nNative ideal-gas transport through a stationary porous medium. Molecular and Knudsen diffusion both include porosity/tortuosity. With permeability=nothing, the Darcy term uses the close-packed-sphere estimate from particle diameter. Provide MultiTransportData to evaluate gas-phase thermal conductivity. The reusable workspace holds diffusion [m²/s], effective binary [m²/s], knudsen [m²/s], and the gas viscosity [Pa s].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.DustyGasTransport"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.EEDFModel","page":"API","text":"EEDFModel\n\nFixed electron-energy grid and collision cross-section tables. Construct a model with read_eedf_model; energies are eV and cross sections are m².\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.EEDFModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.EEDFResult","page":"API","text":"EEDFResult\n\nElectron-energy distribution on edges and centers (eV), electron mobility (m²/(V·s)), and convergence diagnostics. The center distribution satisfies ∫√ε f(ε)dε = 1; edge values are interpolated with endpoint holding and are not renormalized. errors and deltas record the iteration history.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.EEDFResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.EEDFState","page":"API","text":"EEDFState(model; T, P, mole_fractions, molecular_weights, reduced_field, frequency=0, number_density=nothing)\n\nGas conditions for a temporal two-term electron-energy calculation. Temperature is K, pressure Pa, molecular weights kg/kmol, reduced electric field V·m², and frequency Hz. Dictionaries are keyed by collision-target names. Mole fractions are normalized over participating targets; additional species are ignored. number_density is the total gas number density in m⁻³. It defaults to P/(kB*T); supply the actual density for a two-temperature or non-ideal gas.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.EEDFState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.FreeFlame","page":"API","text":"A freely propagating, adiabatic, planar ideal-gas premixed flame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.FreeFlame"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.FreeFlame-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution}","page":"API","text":"FreeFlame(gas; T=300, P=one_atm, X, width=0.03, grid=nothing)\n\nConstruct a native Julia premixed flame with mixture-averaged diffusion using mole-fraction gradients. The inlet mass flux (and flame speed) is an eigenvalue. Initialization uses native constant-enthalpy chemical equilibrium. Call solve! to solve the species and energy equations with adaptive grid refinement. discretization=:finite_difference is the default and matches Cantera's original discretization. :conservative uses shared finite-volume species and total enthalpy fluxes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.FreeFlame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.HeatCapacityDependency","page":"API","text":"HeatCapacityDependency(target, influencing; a, b)\n\nLog-quadratic coverage heat capacity (a*log(T) + b)*theta_j^2 [J/kmol/K]. Its contributions to enthalpy and entropy are integrated analytically and anchored to zero at 298.15 K, following Cantera.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.HeatCapacityDependency"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.HeatTransferWall","page":"API","text":"HeatTransferWall(left, right; area=1.0, U=0.0, heat_flux=0.0)\n\nA fixed wall transferring area*(U*(T_left-T_right)+heat_flux(t)) watts from left to right. Area is in m², U in W/m²/K, and prescribed heat flux in W/m². heat_flux may be a constant or a time function. No wall motion is modeled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.HeatTransferWall"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.IdealGasReactor","page":"API","text":"IdealGasReactor(gas; temperature, pressure=one_atm,\n                mole_fractions=nothing, mass_fractions=nothing,\n                constraint=:constant_pressure, energy=:adiabatic,\n                rate_multipliers=nothing)\n\nA homogeneous, closed ideal-gas reactor. Supply exactly one composition as a species-name dictionary or a vector in mechanism order. Amounts are normalized. constraint is :constant_pressure or :constant_volume; energy is :adiabatic or :isothermal. SI units are used throughout (K, Pa, kg, s).\n\nThe state is [Y₁, …, Yₙ, T]. Constant-pressure reactors conserve specific enthalpy when adiabatic; constant-volume reactors conserve specific internal energy. Isothermal reactors impose the initial temperature. Walls, inlet and outlet flows, surface chemistry, and non-ideal equations of state are excluded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.IdealGasReactor"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.IdealGasThermo","page":"API","text":"Struct for the ideal gas thermo.\n\nnasa_low: Array with low temperature nasa coeff. for each species\n\nnasa_high: Array with high temperature nasa coeff. for each species\n\nTrange: Array with temperature ranges for each species\n\nisTcommon: bool which indicates if both polynoms share same T at intersection\n\nextra: optional NASA9/Shomate/constant-cp regions. The NASA7 coefficient rows are zero for these species; Trange retains their minimum, first split, maximum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.IdealGasThermo"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.IdealGasThermo-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"Constructor for the idealGasThermo:\n\nyaml:: Dict of the input yaml file\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.IdealGasThermo"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.IdealSurface","page":"API","text":"IdealSurface(mechanism; temperature, pressure, mole_fractions, coverages,\n             gas_temperature=temperature)\n\nA surface with fixed temperature, gas pressure, and adjacent gas composition. Coverages denote fractions of occupied sites and sum to one. An optional gas temperature permits direct nonisothermal interface rate evaluation. For a stationary surface equilibrated thermally with its gas, use the default equal temperatures. Persistent solid phases have unit activity and positive inventory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.IdealSurface"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.ImpingingJet","page":"API","text":"An axisymmetric jet impinging on an inert, isothermal surface.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.ImpingingJet"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.ImpingingJet-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution}","page":"API","text":"ImpingingJet(gas; reactants, mdot, T_inlet=300, T_surface=300,\n             P=one_atm, width=.02, initial_products=:equilibrium)\n\nAn axisymmetric jet against an inert isothermal wall. The wall has zero axial velocity and spread rate and zero net species flux. initial_products=:inlet initializes a nonburning flow; :equilibrium initializes a detached flame using native HP equilibrium. Reactive surfaces are not represented by this type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.ImpingingJet"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.InertialWall","page":"API","text":"InertialWall(left, right; area=1, mass, initial_velocity=0, U=0, heat_flux=0)\n\nA piston with one integrated velocity and equation mass*dv/dt = area*(P_left-P_right). Wall mass is in kg and positive velocity expands the left vessel. Gas pressure work transfers energy to the piston's kinetic energy. Heat transfer follows MovingWall. No friction, wall heat capacity, or contact stops are modeled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.InertialWall"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.InterpolativeDependency","page":"API","text":"InterpolativeDependency(target, influencing; enthalpy_coverages, enthalpies,\n                        entropy_coverages, entropies)\n\nTabulated law linearly interpolated between knots. Coverage knots must be strictly increasing, start at 0.0, end at 1.0, and match the value lengths. Enthalpies are in J/kmol, entropies in J/kmol/K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.InterpolativeDependency"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.IonTransportData","page":"API","text":"Model-specific collision fits and charge information for ionized-gas transport.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.IonTransportData"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.IonTransportData-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution}","page":"API","text":"IonTransportData(gas)\n\nPrepare ionized-gas transport from a mechanism preprocessed with mechanism/export_sidecar.py. The collision fits must come from the ionized-gas model. Electron mobility follows that model's fixed 0.4 m²/(V s).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.IonTransportData"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.IonTransportWorkspace","page":"API","text":"Reusable ionized transport storage; binary stores D*P [m² Pa/s], diffusion D [m²/s], mobility [m²/(V s)].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.IonTransportWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.IonizedFlame","page":"API","text":"Planar premixed flame with signed charged species and an algebraic electric field.\n\nConstruct through FreeFlame or BurnerFlame with an ionized-gas mechanism. The initial stage freezes charged diffusion. Enable it with setelectricfield!(flame, true) before the second solve!. Conservative flames first initialize with a frozen finite-difference solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.IonizedFlame"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.KineticsWorkspace","page":"API","text":"Reusable arrays for allocation-free kinetics evaluation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.KineticsWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.LinearDependency","page":"API","text":"LinearDependency(target, influencing; enthalpy=0.0, entropy=0.0)\n\nLinear lateral-interaction law. Adds enthalpy*theta_j [J/kmol] and entropy*theta_j [J/kmol/K] to the standard state of species target, where j = influencing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.LinearDependency"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.LogRateData","page":"API","text":"Log-domain Arrhenius parameters for mixed-precision rate evaluation.\n\nThe outer thermochemical state and source accumulation retain their existing precision. Only the stored Arrhenius parameters and elementary exponential use T, which is typically Float32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.LogRateData"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.MassFlowController","page":"API","text":"MassFlowController(upstream, downstream; mdot)\n\nOne-way flow in kg/s, independent of pressure. Node identifiers are symbols. mdot is a number, f(t), or f(states, t), where named node states expose mass, temperature, pressure, density, and mass_fractions. For example, mdot=(states,t)->states.combustor.mass/0.1 sets a 0.1 s residence time. Negative requested rates are clipped to zero. Treat callback states as read-only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.MassFlowController"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.MixtureThermalDiffusionWorkspace","page":"API","text":"Reusable storage for the separate mixture-averaged Soret model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.MixtureThermalDiffusionWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.MovingWall","page":"API","text":"MovingWall(left, right; area=1, K=0, velocity=0, U=0, heat_flux=0)\n\nA massless piston between named network nodes. Positive velocity expands the left vessel and compresses the right. Its velocity is K*(P_left-P_right) + velocity(states,t), in m/s; prescribed velocity may also be a number or f(t). Heat flowing left to right is area*(U*(T_left-T_right) + heat_flux(t)), in W.\n\nPressure work uses each vessel's own pressure. Consequently a pressure-driven massless piston removes mechanical energy from the gases at area*velocity*(P_left-P_right); this work is recorded by the network's ledger. The piston has neither inertia nor a stored thermal energy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.MovingWall"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.MovingWallNetwork","page":"API","text":"MovingWallNetwork(network::ReactorNetwork; walls)\n\nAdd moving walls to an ideal-gas network, retaining its chemistry, flow devices, and fixed heat-transfer walls. Reservoirs remain fixed boundaries. State order: the base network's species masses/temperatures, vessel volumes, inertial-wall velocities, then cumulative external mass input, energy input (including isothermal thermostats), and gas pressure work output. Use moving_wall_state and moving_wall_diagnostics instead of interpreting indices directly.\n\nAll runtime calculations are Julia. Callers supply a stiff ODE integrator to solve_moving_wall. At a prescribed discontinuity, restart with each segment's continuous wall law; example/reactors/piston.jl demonstrates this for a release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.MovingWallNetwork"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.MultiTransportData","page":"API","text":"Temperature-independent neutral ideal-gas collision fits and species constants.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.MultiTransportData"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.MultiTransportWorkspace","page":"API","text":"Reusable storage. diffusion is m²/s; thermal_diffusion is kg/(m s); binary stores pressure times diffusivity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.MultiTransportWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.PiecewiseLinearDependency","page":"API","text":"PiecewiseLinearDependency(target, influencing; enthalpy_low, enthalpy_high,\n                          enthalpy_change, entropy_low, entropy_high,\n                          entropy_change)\n\nTwo-segment law with slopes *_low below the change coverage and *_high above it, continuous at *_change. Slopes are in J/kmol (enthalpy) or J/kmol/K (entropy); change coverages are dimensionless in (0, 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.PiecewiseLinearDependency"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.PlasmaEnergyReactor","page":"API","text":"PlasmaEnergyReactor(state; volume=1.0)\n\nA closed, constant-pressure Boltzmann-plasma reactor. Its state is [mass, total_enthalpy, mass_fractions...]; electron temperature and the electric field/EEDF are held fixed between explicit update_eedf! calls on the prepared RHS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.PlasmaEnergyReactor"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.PlasmaMechanism","page":"API","text":"PlasmaMechanism(path; phase=nothing, data_paths=String[], atomic_weights=Dict())\n\nRead an ideal plasma mechanism directly from Cantera-style YAML. Imported species files are searched beside the mechanism and in data_paths. Supports isotropic rates and Boltzmann two-term distributions. Boltzmann phases also support imported gas thermodynamics, reversible Arrhenius reactions, falloff and Chebyshev rates. Reaction orders are nonnegative integers. Rates use kmol, m, s, K and J; electron energies are in eV.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.PlasmaMechanism"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.PlasmaReactor","page":"API","text":"PlasmaReactor(state)\n\nA homogeneous constant-pressure reactor with fixed gas and electron temperatures. Snapshots the supplied state; later state setters do not modify this reactor. The ODE state contains species mass fractions only. Chemical energy evolution, electric-field coupling and evolving EEDF are not part of this isothermal model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.PlasmaReactor"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.PlasmaState","page":"API","text":"PlasmaState(mechanism; temperature, pressure, mole_fractions, mass_fractions,\n            mean_electron_energy)\n\nAn ideal plasma state with a separate electron temperature. Defaults come from its YAML phase; specify at most one composition. Input fractions are normalized. Temperatures are K, pressure Pa, density kg/m³ and mean electron energy eV. Use set_mean_electron_energy! to change electron energy at fixed density.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.PlasmaState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.PolynomialDependency","page":"API","text":"PolynomialDependency(target, influencing; enthalpy_coefficients=zeros(4),\n                     entropy_coefficients=zeros(4))\n\nQuartic polynomial law sum_i c_i*theta_j^i for orders one through four. Enthalpy coefficients are in J/kmol, entropy coefficients in J/kmol/K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.PolynomialDependency"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.PressureController","page":"API","text":"PressureController(upstream, downstream; primary, K, pressure_function=identity)\n\nOne-way flow equal to the primary device's flow plus K*pressure_function(ΔP). primary is an earlier device in the network's flow tuple, or its one-based index. K has units kg/s/Pa for the default pressure function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.PressureController"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.PureWater","page":"API","text":"PureWater()\n\nReynolds liquid/vapor water equation of state, evaluated entirely in Julia. Temperatures span 273.16–1600 K; saturation spans 273.16–647.286 K. This model does not describe ice. Thermochemical reference offsets match Cantera's water phase. A model holds a saturation cache; use one model per concurrent task.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.PureWater"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.ReactorNetwork","page":"API","text":"ReactorNetwork(nodes::NamedTuple; flows=(), walls=())\n\nConnect named WellStirredReactors and Reservoirs with one-way flow devices and fixed heat-transfer walls. Species are mapped by name. Every species that can enter a vessel must exist in its mechanism; incompatible molecular weights are rejected. Reservoir outlets need not contain every exhaust species.\n\nAll calculations use ideal-gas thermodynamics. This network supports fixed volumes, homogeneous chemistry, mixing, pressure regulation, and heat transfer. Surface chemistry, moving walls, and non-ideal phases are excluded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.ReactorNetwork"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.ReactorRHS","page":"API","text":"A reusable in-place ODE function. Use a separate instance for each concurrent solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.ReactorRHS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.ReactorSurface","page":"API","text":"ReactorSurface(node, mechanism; area, coverages=mechanism.initial_coverages)\n\nAttach a fixed-area ideal surface to an isothermal WellStirredReactor named node. The surface follows that reactor's temperature, pressure, and gas composition. Persistent bulk products are tracked as signed deposited amounts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.ReactorSurface"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.RealGasKineticsWorkspace","page":"API","text":"Reusable native RK property, physical concentration and activity arrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.RealGasKineticsWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.RedlichKwongReactor","page":"API","text":"RedlichKwongReactor(gas, model; temperature, pressure=one_atm,\n                   mole_fractions=nothing, mass_fractions=nothing,\n                   rate_multipliers=nothing)\n\nClosed, constant-volume, adiabatic homogeneous reactor with native RK thermodynamics and native reaction rates. gas supplies kinetic sidecar data; its species order and molecular weights must match model. Supply exactly one composition. State variables are [Y₁,…,Yₙ,T], in SI units. The energy equation uses the constant-T,V derivative of total energy with respect to each species' mole amount, rather than its ordinary partial molar internal energy. Elementary Arrhenius, third-body, Lindemann, Troe and PLOG reactions are supported. Blowers–Masel rates require additional nonideal enthalpy coupling and are rejected by this constructor.\n\nUse the existing caller-owned ODE interface solve_reactor(...; integrator). No ODE package or Cantera runtime is required by the core implementation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.RedlichKwongReactor"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.RedlichKwongThermo","page":"API","text":"RedlichKwongThermo(yaml_or_path; phase=nothing, molecular_weights=nothing,\n                  critical_properties=nothing)\n\nNative single-phase Redlich–Kwong mixture model. Species use the existing ideal reference thermodynamics and a(T)=a0+a1*T, quadratic attraction mixing and linear covolume mixing. Explicit binary-a coefficients override geometric means. YAML units and species critical parameters are accepted. An optional critical-properties YAML file supplies missing critical data; none are guessed. Pure-species a0 and b must be nonnegative; a1 and binary coefficients may be signed. Unlike-species coefficients use the nonnegative geometric mean while pure-species a1 retains its sign. Opposite-sign a1 pairs require explicit binary-a coefficients because their geometric mean is not real.\n\nThe standalone constructor needs no kinetic sidecar or Cantera installation. Default atomic weights cover H, He, C, N, O, S, Cl and Ar. For other elements or isotopes, supply molecular weights in kg/kmol. No phase-equilibrium or Peng–Robinson calculation is implied by this model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.RedlichKwongThermo"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.RedlichKwongWorkspace","page":"API","text":"Reusable property arrays; use one workspace per concurrent caller.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.RedlichKwongWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.Reservoir","page":"API","text":"A constant-temperature, constant-pressure ideal-gas boundary for a reactor network.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.Reservoir"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.StoichiometricCondensedPhase","page":"API","text":"StoichiometricCondensedPhase(; name, species, elements, molecular_weight,\n    molar_volume, thermo_model, thermo_coefficients, temperature_range,\n    reference_pressure=one_atm, charge=0.0)\nStoichiometricCondensedPhase(path)\n\nA pure condensed phase of fixed stoichiometry and constant molar volume, in equilibrium with one ideal gas through equilibrate(gas, phase; ...). The phase is a single neutral species with NASA7 or constant-cp standard thermodynamics; it is not a general multiphase, nonideal, or electrochemical phase model. All inputs are SI.\n\nKeyword arguments:\n\nname, species: phase and species names.\nelements: dictionary of element names to atom counts, e.g. Dict(\"C\" => 1.0) for graphite. Counts must be finite and nonnegative with at least one positive; negative (charged) counts are rejected.\nmolecular_weight: kg/kmol, positive and finite.\nmolar_volume: m^3/kmol, positive and finite. Enters the incompressible pressure correction h(T, P) = h0(T) + (P - reference_pressure) * molar_volume with h in J/kmol.\nthermo_model: \"NASA7\" or \"constant-cp\".\nthermo_coefficients: NASA7: [Tmid, a1..a7 high-temperature region, a1..a7 low-temperature region] giving h in J/kmol and s in J/kmol/K; constant-cp: [T0, h0, s0, cp0] giving h = h0 + cp0*(T - T0) and s = s0 + cp0*log(T/T0).\ntemperature_range: [Tmin, Tmax] kelvin declaring polynomial validity. NASA7 requires finite positive bounds with Tmid strictly inside. Constant-cp permits zero and Inf as open fit bounds; temperatures must still be positive. The JSON representation uses null for an infinite upper bound.\nreference_pressure: Pa, positive, default one atmosphere.\ncharge: must be zero; charged species are unsupported.\n\nThe path constructor loads the numeric JSON written by mechanism/export_condensed_phase.py (format arrhenius-condensed-phase-v1); the same validations are applied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.StoichiometricCondensedPhase"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.SurfaceFlowReactor","page":"API","text":"SurfaceFlowReactor(surface; temperature, pressure=one_atm, mole_fractions,\n                   area, mass_flow_rate, surface_area_per_length)\n\nAn isothermal, constant-area, frictionless ideal-gas plug-flow reactor. Surface coverages satisfy stationary algebraic equations at every axial position. surface_area_per_length is catalyst area per axial metre, in m²/m. Persistent bulk phases are not supported here: total gas mass flux remains constant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.SurfaceFlowReactor"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.SurfaceMechanism","page":"API","text":"SurfaceMechanism(path)\n\nLoad parameters prepared by mechanism/export_surface.py. Supports one ideal surface, one ideal gas, and persistent fixed-stoichiometry solids; NASA7 and constant-cp species; elementary and sticking Arrhenius reactions, explicit orders, Motz–Wise corrections, and polynomial coverage activation energies. Species are ordered surface, gas, then bulk. Rates use kmol, m, s, K, and J.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.SurfaceMechanism"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.SurfaceWorkspace","page":"API","text":"A reusable surface rate workspace. Returned rate arrays alias this workspace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.SurfaceWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.TwoTermOptions","page":"API","text":"TwoTermOptions(; delta0=1e14, max_iterations=200, factor=4., rtol=1e-5,\n               initial_kTe=2., positivity_floor=1e-300, low_field_threshold=1e-21)\n\nIteration controls for solve_eedf. initial_kTe is eV and the field threshold is V·m². The positivity floor regularizes logarithmic slopes only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.TwoTermOptions"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.Valve","page":"API","text":"A one-way valve: mdot = max(0, K * timefunction(t) * pressurefunction(ΔP)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.Valve"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.WaterState","page":"API","text":"Water state in SI units: T [K], P [Pa], rho [kg/m³], h/u [J/kg], s [J/kg/K].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.WaterState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Arrhenius.WellStirredReactor","page":"API","text":"WellStirredReactor(initial; volume=1.0, chemistry=true)\nWellStirredReactor(gas; volume=1.0, chemistry=true, kwargs...)\n\nA homogeneous fixed-volume vessel, with volume in m³. Its state is the species masses in kg followed by temperature in K. Total mass and mass fractions are derived from species masses. The initial IdealGasReactor must use :constant_volume; the gas constructor selects that constraint automatically. Energy may be adiabatic or isothermal. Set chemistry=false for pure mixing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.WellStirredReactor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.C2X-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"get mole fraction (X) from concentration (C)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.C2X"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.CreateSolution-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"CreateSolution(mech; data_paths=String[], phase=nothing)\n\nLoad the first gas phase, or the phase named phase with sidecar <mech>.<phase>.npz, from a YAML mechanism and its preprocessed NPZ sidecar. Imported species/reaction sections require a v4 sidecar. Imports are resolved relative to the mechanism first, then in data_paths. Thermochemistry and kinetics are evaluated in Julia after loading.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.CreateSolution"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.H_mass_func-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"get enthaphy (H) per mass\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.H_mass_func"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.S_mass_func-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"get entropy (S) per unit mass\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.S_mass_func"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.U_mass_func-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"get enthaphy (U) per mass\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.U_mass_func"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.X2C-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"get concentration (C) from mole fraction (X)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.X2C"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.X2Y-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"get mass fraction (Y) from mole fraction (X)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.X2Y"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.Y2C-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"get concentration (C) from mass fraction (Y)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.Y2C"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.Y2X-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"get mole fraction (X) from mass fraction (Y)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.Y2X"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.activation_energy-Tuple{BlowersMaselRate, Any}","page":"API","text":"Effective activation energy [J/kmol] at the supplied reaction enthalpy [J/kmol].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.activation_energy"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_a-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_a(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the molar helmholz free energy (a) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_a"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_a_RT-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, IdealGasThermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_a_RT(gas, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the dimensionless mole based helmholz free energy (a) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_a_RT"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_a_mean-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_a_mean(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the mean mole based helmholz free energy (a) of the mixture\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_a_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_amass-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_amass(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the partial mass based helmholz free energy (a) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_amass"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_amass_mean-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_amass_mean(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the mean mass based helmholz free energy (a) of the mixture\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_amass_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_cp-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_cp(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the molar Heat capacity at constant pressure (cp) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_cp"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_cp_R-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, IdealGasThermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_cp_R(gas, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the dimensionless mole based heat capacity  at constant pressure (cp) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_cp_R"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_cp_mean-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_cp_mean(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the mean mole based Heat capacity at constant pressure (cp) of the mixture\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_cp_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_cpmass-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_cpmass(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the partial mass based Heat capacity at constant pressure (cp) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_cpmass"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_cpmass_mean-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_cpmass_mean(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the mean mass based Heat capacity at constant pressure (cp) of the mixture\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_cpmass_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_cv-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_cv(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the molar Heat capacity at constant volume (cv) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_cv"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_cv_R-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, IdealGasThermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_cv_R(gas, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the dimensionless mole based heat capacity  at constant volume (cv) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_cv_R"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_cv_mean-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_cv_mean(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the mean mole based Heat capacity at constant volume (cv) of the mixture\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_cv_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_cvmass-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_cvmass(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the partial mass based Heat capacity at constant volume (cv) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_cvmass"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_cvmass_mean-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_cvmass_mean(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the mean mass based Heat capacity at constant volume (cv) of the mixture\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_cvmass_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_g-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_g(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the molar gibbs free energy (g) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_g"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_g_RT-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, IdealGasThermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_g_RT(gas, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the dimensionless mole based free gibbs energy (g) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_g_RT"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_g_mean-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_g_mean(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the mean mole based gibbs free energy (g) of the mixture\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_g_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_gmass-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_gmass(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the partial mass based gibbs free energy (g) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_gmass"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_gmass_mean-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_gmass_mean(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the mean mass based gibbs free energy (g) of the mixture\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_gmass_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_h-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_h(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the molar enthalpy (h) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_h"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_h_RT-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, IdealGasThermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_h_RT(gas, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the dimensionless mole based enthalpy (h) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_h_RT"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_h_cp_s0!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Arrhenius.Solution, IdealGasThermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"Species hRT, cpR, and s0R in one pass; element values match the individual `cal*_R!` functions term for term.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_h_cp_s0!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_h_mean-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_h_mean(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the mean mole based enthalpy (h) of the mixture\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_h_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_hmass-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_hmass(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the partial mass based enthalpy (h) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_hmass"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_hmass_mean-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_hmass_mean(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the mean mass based enthalpy (h) of the mixture\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_hmass_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_s-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_s(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the molar entropy (s) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_s"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_s0-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_s0(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the molar reference entropy (s0) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_s0"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_s0_R-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, IdealGasThermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_s0_R(gas, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the dimensionless mole based reference state entropy (s0) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_s0_R"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_s0_mean-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_s0_mean(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the mean mole based reference entropy (s0) of the mixture\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_s0_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_s0mass-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_s0mass(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the partial mass based reference entropy (s0) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_s0mass"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_s0mass_mean-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_s0mass_mean(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the mean mass based reference entropy (s0) of the mixture\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_s0mass_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_s_R-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, IdealGasThermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_s_R(gas, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the dimensionless mole based entropy (s) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_s_R"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_s_mean-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_s_mean(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the mean mole based entropy (s) of the mixture\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_s_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_smass-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_smass(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the partial mass based entropy (s) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_smass"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_smass_mean-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_smass_mean(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the mean mass based entropy (s) of the mixture\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_smass_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_u-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_u(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the molar internal energy for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_u"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_u_RT-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, IdealGasThermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_u_RT(gas, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the dimensionless mole based internal energy (u) for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_u_RT"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_u_mean-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_u_mean(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the mean mole based internal energy of the mixture\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_u_mean"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_umass-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_umass(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the partial mass based internal energy for each species\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_umass"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.cal_umass_mean-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Arrhenius.Thermo, Real, Real, AbstractArray}","page":"API","text":"cal_umass_mean(Solution, T, p, X)\n\ncalculates the mean mass based internal energy of the mixture\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.cal_umass_mean"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Arrhenius.catalytic_diagnostics","page":"API","text":"Combined inventory and conservation rates, including surface storage and bulk deposition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.catalytic_diagnostics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.catalytic_wall_diagnostics-Tuple{CatalyticImpingingJet}","page":"API","text":"Surface rates, wall mass fluxes and elemental balances in SI units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.catalytic_wall_diagnostics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.convert_precision-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Type{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"API","text":"convert_precision(gas::Solution, T)\n\nReturn an independent copy of gas whose floating-point mechanism, thermodynamic, transport, and molecular data use type T. Index arrays and species names retain their original types. The default YAML loader continues to construct Float64 solutions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.convert_precision"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Arrhenius.counterflow_residual!","page":"API","text":"Evaluate continuity, radial momentum, pressure curvature, species and energy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.counterflow_residual!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.coverage_thermo!-Tuple{CoverageThermoWorkspace, CoverageThermoModel, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"coverage_thermo!(workspace, model, T, theta)\n\nEvaluate coverage-dependent standard-state properties at temperature T [K] and coverages theta (must lie in [0, 1] and sum to one). Fills workspace.enthalpy_RT (h°/RT), workspace.entropy_R (s°/R, including the +log(theta_ref) standard-state reference-coverage correction), workspace.cp_R (cp°/R), and workspace.gibbs_RT (g°/RT = h°/RT - s°/R). The returned arrays alias the workspace.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.coverage_thermo!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.coverage_thermo-Tuple{CoverageThermoModel, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"coverage_thermo(model, T, theta)\n\nAllocating convenience form of coverage_thermo!; returns a NamedTuple of independently owned arrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.coverage_thermo"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.critical_properties-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"critical_properties(fluid::AbstractString)\n\nReturn the TPX model's critical temperature T [K], pressure P [Pa], density density [kg/m³], molecular weight molecular_weight [kg/kmol], and critical compressibility Z = P*molecular_weight/(density*R*T).\n\nSupported names (case insensitive) are water, nitrogen, methane, hydrogen, oxygen, carbon dioxide, heptane, and HFC-134a. This function provides critical properties; it does not construct an equation-of-state model. For liquid/vapor water states, use PureWater.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.critical_properties"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.density-Tuple{Arrhenius.AbstractPremixedFlame}","page":"API","text":"Mass density [kg/m³] at each grid point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.density"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.disable_two_point_control!-Tuple{CounterflowDiffusionFlame}","page":"API","text":"disable_two_point_control!(flame)\n\nRemove the auxiliary mass-flux row, store the current axial mass-flux endpoints [kg/(m² s)] as the inlet fluxes and restore inlet-flux boundary conditions. Enable/disable without solving reproduces the initial state bit-exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.disable_two_point_control!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.dusty_gas_diffusion!-Tuple{DustyGasTransport, Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"dusty_gas_diffusion!(workspace, P, T, X)\n\nReturn the full dusty-gas diffusion matrix [m²/s]. P is Pa, T is K, and X contains normalized mole fractions. The returned matrix belongs to the workspace and is replaced by the next coefficient or flux evaluation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.dusty_gas_diffusion!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.dusty_gas_molar_fluxes!-Tuple{Any, DustyGasTransport, Vararg{Any, 7}}","page":"API","text":"dusty_gas_molar_fluxes!(flux, workspace, T1, T2, rho1, rho2, Y1, Y2, delta)\n\nWrite species molar fluxes [kmol/(m² s)] from point 1 toward point 2, separated by positive delta [m]. Temperatures are K, densities kg/m³, and Y1, Y2 are normalized mass fractions. The calculation includes molecular diffusion, Knudsen diffusion and pressure-driven Darcy flow. No zero-total-flux correction is applied: momentum can be transferred to the stationary porous medium.\n\nCoefficients are evaluated at arithmetic mean T/P and mole fractions formed from mean endpoint concentrations. Darcy's term uses those mean concentrations directly, including when endpoint temperatures differ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.dusty_gas_molar_fluxes!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.dusty_gas_molar_fluxes-Tuple{DustyGasTransport, Vararg{Any}}","page":"API","text":"Allocate and return species molar fluxes [kmol/(m² s)] between two endpoint states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.dusty_gas_molar_fluxes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.dusty_gas_permeability-Tuple{DustyGasTransport}","page":"API","text":"The prescribed or close-packed-sphere Darcy permeability [m²].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.dusty_gas_permeability"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.dusty_gas_thermal_conductivity-Tuple{DustyGasTransport, Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"Gas-phase multicomponent thermal conductivity [W/(m K)]; no porous-solid correction is included.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.dusty_gas_thermal_conductivity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.equilibrate-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, StoichiometricCondensedPhase}","page":"API","text":"equilibrate(gas::Solution, phase::StoichiometricCondensedPhase; T,\n    P=one_atm, X, mode=:TP, initial_gas_moles=1.0,\n    temperature_bounds=(200.0, 6000.0), property_rtol=1e-10)\n\nNative equilibrium of one ideal gas with a single initially absent fixed-stoichiometry condensed phase. Supported conserved pairs are :TP and :HP (symbols or strings); the gas-only equilibrate methods and their tolerances are unchanged. The initial state is initial_gas_moles kmol of gas at T, P, X plus zero kmol of the condensed phase; initially present solids are not supported, and neither are multiple condensed phases, nonideal phases, or charged species.\n\nThe condensed phase forms only when its element inventory allows it and its formation affinity is positive. If the phase requires an element with zero initial inventory, formation is excluded by conservation: the gas-only state is returned with zero condensed amount, status == :inactive_by_elements, and affinity == NaN.\n\nReturns (; T, P, X, Y, gas_moles, condensed_moles, gas_species_moles, species_moles, status, affinity, element_error, enthalpy_error): gas mole and mass fractions X/Y, the total gas amount gas_moles (kmol), the condensed amount condensed_moles (kmol), the per-species gas amounts, the combined amounts (gas species order, then the condensed species), a status of :gas_only, :condensed_present, or :inactive_by_elements, the dimensionless phase affinity (zero when present, nonpositive when absent), the relative all-element conservation error, and the relative enthalpy conservation error (NaN for :TP).\n\nThe system is solved per 1 kmol of initial gas and all amounts are scaled by initial_gas_moles; equilibrium temperatures and compositions do not depend on it. temperature_bounds brackets the :HP temperature search; like the gas-only solver, evaluation outside the species fit ranges extrapolates the polynomials. property_rtol controls the :HP conserved-enthalpy solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.equilibrate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.equilibrate-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution}","page":"API","text":"equilibrate(gas; T, P=one_atm, X, mode=:TP, temperature_bounds=(200,6000),\n            property_rtol=1e-10)\n\nNative single-phase ideal-gas equilibrium. Supported conserved pairs are :TP, :TV, :HP, :UV, :SP and :SV (symbols or strings). The supplied state defines the conserved specific enthalpy, internal energy, entropy, or volume. Returns (T, P, X, Y); no input is modified. Absent ordinary elements stay absent; signed electron counts conserve the supplied net charge.\n\nThe temperature search uses the explicit temperature_bounds. Like the underlying NASA property functions, evaluation outside species fit ranges extrapolates the polynomials. Supply bounds within the shared fit interval when extrapolation is unsuitable. This solver does not support nonideal, surface, or multiphase equilibrium. property_rtol controls the outer conserved-property temperature solve for HP, UV, SP and SV states, with an enthalpy/energy scale floor of 1e6 J/kg or an entropy scale floor of 1e3 J/(kg K). Element-balance tolerances are unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.equilibrate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.equilibrium_sound_speeds-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution}","page":"API","text":"equilibrium_sound_speeds(gas; T, P=one_atm, X, pressure_step=1e-4,\n                        temperature_bounds=(200,6000))\n\nEquilibrate at (T,P) and estimate equilibrium and frozen sound speeds from a relative pressure perturbation at constant entropy. Returns speeds in m/s as equilibrium, frozen, and frozen_at_equilibrium, plus the unperturbed state and perturbed_state. The last speed uses the analytic frozen formula at the perturbed equilibrium state. The pressure derivative has first-order truncation error; pressure_step must also be large enough to resolve equilibrium errors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.equilibrium_sound_speeds"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.equivalence_ratio-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Any}","page":"API","text":"equivalence_ratio(gas, X; fuel=nothing, oxidizer=nothing, basis=:mole,\n                  include_species=nothing)\n\nReturn equivalence ratio from a mole composition. With explicit streams, use the conserved Bilger mixture fraction. Without streams, assume all C/H/S originate in fuel and all O in oxidizer. Optionally restrict to named species.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.equivalence_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.extinct-Tuple{CounterflowDiffusionFlame}","page":"API","text":"Whether the computed temperature rise is less than 10 K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.extinct"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Arrhenius.flame_residual!","page":"API","text":"Evaluate the discretized steady species, energy, and mass-flow residual.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.flame_residual!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.flame_speed_sensitivities-Tuple{FreeFlame}","page":"API","text":"flame_speed_sensitivities(f::FreeFlame)\n\nNormalized laminar flame speed sensitivities s_i = (k_iS_u)dS_udk_i to the rate constant of every reaction at a converged conservative free flame. Construct with discretization=:conservative and solve before calling. One adjoint solve with the steady Newton Jacobian gives λ with Jᵀλ = S_uu; scaling reaction i scales its net rate of progress, which enters only the species balances of each node, so s_i = -λᵀ Fln k_i  S_u. The adjoint uses a finite-difference Jacobian with transport coefficients updated in every column, as Cantera's adjoint sensitivities do.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.flame_speed_sensitivities"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.frozen_sound_speed-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution}","page":"API","text":"frozen_sound_speed(gas; T, P=one_atm, X)\n\nIdeal-gas sound speed in m/s with composition held fixed, sqrt((cp/cv)*R*T/MW).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.frozen_sound_speed"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.get_H-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"API","text":"get enthaphy (H) per mole\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.get_H"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.get_S-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"API","text":"get entropy (S)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.get_S"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.get_U-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"get enthaphy (U) per mole\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.get_U"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.get_cp-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"API","text":"get specific of heat capacity\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.get_cp"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.get_cv-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"get specific of heat capacity\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.get_cv"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.heat_release_rate-Tuple{Arrhenius.AbstractPremixedFlame}","page":"API","text":"Volumetric chemical heat release rate [W/m³], positive for heat release.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.heat_release_rate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.heat_release_rate-Tuple{Union{Arrhenius.AbstractPremixedCounterflow, CounterflowDiffusionFlame}}","page":"API","text":"heat_release_rate(f)\n\nVolumetric chemical heat release rate [W/m^3] at each grid point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.heat_release_rate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.initialize_catalytic_coverages!-Tuple{CatalyticImpingingJet}","page":"API","text":"Initialize stationary coverages at the inlet composition and wall temperature. integrator is a caller-owned stiff native Julia surface ODE integrator, as in solve_surface. This initialization never uses a reference flow solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.initialize_catalytic_coverages!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.ionized_flux!-Tuple{AbstractVector, IonTransportWorkspace, IonTransportData, Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"ionized_flux!(flux, workspace, data, gradX, Yleft, Yright;\n              density, electric_field=0.0, frozen=false)\n\nFill mass fluxes [kg/(m² s)] with midpoint transport and mole-fraction gradients. The density is the left-node density; drift uses the average of endpoint mass fractions. The electric-field stage corrects neutral fluxes to conserve total mass. The frozen stage sets charged fluxes to zero and applies the source neutral correction without renormalizing neutral mass fractions. Endpoint mass fractions may contain signed, unnormalized Newton trial values; they are not clipped or normalized by this algebraic flux function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.ionized_flux!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.ionized_transport!-Tuple{IonTransportWorkspace, IonTransportData, Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"ionized_transport!(workspace, data, P, T, X)\n\nReturn mixture viscosity [Pa s], thermal conductivity [W/(m K)], and electrical conductivity [S/m], and update diffusion coefficients and mobilities. Input mole fractions sum to one; signed solver traces are floored only in the transport sums, following Cantera. All properties use gas temperature. The optional meanmolecularweight preserves a solver state's molecular weight when its underlying mass fractions are not normalized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.ionized_transport!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.isentropic_state-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution}","page":"API","text":"isentropic_state(gas; T, P=one_atm, X, pressure,\n                 temperature_bounds=(1,6000))\n\nFind the state at pressure (Pa) with the entropy and composition of (T,P,X). Returns (T,P,X,Y) without changing the input. Species heat capacities may vary with temperature. The explicit temperature bounds constrain the search; values outside species fit ranges extrapolate their thermodynamic polynomials.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.isentropic_state"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.mass_fractions-Tuple{Arrhenius.AbstractPremixedFlame}","page":"API","text":"Species mass fractions, with species along rows and grid points along columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.mass_fractions"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.mass_fractions-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Any}","page":"API","text":"Return normalized mass fractions, interpreting input on the specified basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.mass_fractions"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.mix_constant_pressure-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Any}","page":"API","text":"mix_constant_pressure(gas::Solution, streams)\n\nSteady adiabatic mixing of ideal-gas streams at a common pressure. streams is a tuple or vector of named tuples, each with T (K), P (Pa), X (composition) and moles (kmol). Every stream shares gas — one species ordering and ideal-gas thermodynamics — and one pressure, matched to a relative tolerance of 1e-10. Compositions accept any form mole_fractions supports. Stream amounts must be finite and nonnegative with a positive total; streams carrying zero amount do not participate in the mixture. Inputs are never modified.\n\nThe mixed composition and totals follow from species and mass conservation. The mixed temperature conserves the total enthalpy, sum(moles * cal_h_mean(gas, T, P, X)) in J, with molar enthalpies in J/kmol from the species thermodynamic models; their reference states fix the energy convention. A safeguarded Newton solve with derivative cal_cp_mean brackets the root between the inlet temperature extremes; equal inlet temperatures return that exact temperature. Temperatures and amounts are Float64, matching the IdealGasStates APIs; the root solve itself does not support generic automatic differentiation.\n\nReturns (T, P, X, Y, moles, mass, enthalpy): K, Pa, mole and mass fractions, total kmol, total mass in kg and the conserved total enthalpy in J. The state stays chemically frozen — call equilibrate separately to equilibrate it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.mix_constant_pressure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.mix_trans-Union{Tuple{A}, Tuple{A, Vararg{Any, 4}}} where A<:Arrhenius.Solution","page":"API","text":"mix_trans(gas::A, P, T, X, mean_MW) where {A <: Arrhenius.Solution}\n\nCompute the tranposrt properties of a mixture using mixture average formula\n\nEquations Ref. https://personal.ems.psu.edu/~radovic/ChemKinTheoryPaSR.pdf Equations. 5-50/51/52 for viscosity and thermal conductivity\n\nPure species viscosities [Pa-s]\n\nThermal conductivity. [W/m/K].\n\nEquation 5-46 for diffusion\n\nMixture-averaged diffusion coefficients [m^2/s] relating the mass-averaged diffusive fluxes  (with respect to the mass averaged velocity) to gradients in the species mole fractions.\n\nTest this module in transporttest.jl\n\nSee also implementations in ReacTorch\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.mix_trans"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.mixture_fraction-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Any}","page":"API","text":"mixture_fraction(gas, X; fuel, oxidizer, basis=:mole, element=\"Bilger\")\n\nReturn fuel mass fraction inferred from conserved elements. X is a mole composition; basis applies only to fuel and oxidizer. The default Bilger definition combines C, H, S and O; an individual element may also be selected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.mixture_fraction"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.mixture_thermal_diffusion!-Tuple{MixtureThermalDiffusionWorkspace, MultiTransportData, Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"mixture_thermal_diffusion!(workspace, data, P, T, X)\n\nCompute the mixture-averaged Soret coefficients [kg/(m s)], following Cantera 4 MixTransport::getThermalDiffCoeffs. Return the updated vector workspace.thermal_diffusion. These are distinct from multicomponent Soret coefficients; their mass-flux contribution is -DT * grad_T / T for either mole- or mass-gradient mixture diffusion. Requires the collision sidecar, but no species heat capacities or coupled multicomponent matrix solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.mixture_thermal_diffusion!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.mixture_transport!-Tuple{TransportWorkspace, Arrhenius.Solution, Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"mixture_transport!(workspace, gas, P, T, X)\n\nReturn viscosity [Pa s] and conductivity [W/(m K)], and fill workspace.diffusion with mole-gradient mixture diffusion coefficients [m²/s]. X must contain normalized mole fractions. Requires transport polynomials from mechanism/export_sidecar.py.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.mixture_transport!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.mole_fractions-Tuple{Arrhenius.AbstractPremixedFlame}","page":"API","text":"Species mole fractions, with species along rows and grid points along columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.mole_fractions"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.mole_fractions-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Any}","page":"API","text":"mole_fractions(gas, composition; basis=:mole)\n\nReturn normalized mole fractions. Compositions may be vectors, species-name dictionaries, \"H2:2, O2:1\", or a single species name. basis=:mass interprets the input amounts as masses. The input is never modified.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.mole_fractions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Arrhenius.moving_wall_diagnostics","page":"API","text":"moving_wall_diagnostics(model_or_rhs, u, t=0)\n\nReport vessel states, wall velocity/heat/pressure work, and integrated balances. mass_balance = total_mass - external_mass_input and energy_balance = total_internal_energy + pressure_work_output - energy_input are invariants. Heat and work are signed in J, masses in kg, and elemental inventories in kmol. Internal gas heat transfers cancel from energy input. wall_kinetic_energy accounts for inertial walls separately; massless piston work need not vanish, even for an otherwise closed network.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.moving_wall_diagnostics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.moving_wall_isoutofdomain-Tuple{MovingWallNetwork, Any}","page":"API","text":"Reject nonpositive volume, temperature, and mass, or materially negative species mass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.moving_wall_isoutofdomain"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Arrhenius.moving_wall_jacobian!","page":"API","text":"Dense finite-difference Jacobian with species, temperature, volume, and velocity couplings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.moving_wall_jacobian!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.moving_wall_problem-Tuple{MovingWallNetwork, Any}","page":"API","text":"Prepare native callbacks for a caller-owned stiff ODE integrator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.moving_wall_problem"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.moving_wall_state-Tuple{MovingWallNetwork}","page":"API","text":"Independent initial species-mass, temperature, volume, velocity, and ledger state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.moving_wall_state"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.multicomponent_fluxes!-Tuple{Any, MultiTransportWorkspace, MultiTransportData, Vararg{Any, 5}}","page":"API","text":"multicomponent_fluxes!(flux, workspace, data, P, T, X, grad_X, grad_T)\n\nReturn mass fluxes [kg/(m² s)] relative to the mass-averaged velocity, using already updated transport coefficients. grad_X is in 1/m and grad_T in K/m. The Soret contribution is -thermal_diffusion * grad_T / T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.multicomponent_fluxes!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.multicomponent_thermal_conductivity!-Tuple{MultiTransportWorkspace, MultiTransportData, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"API","text":"multicomponent_thermal_conductivity!(workspace, data, gas, P, T, X)\nmulticomponent_thermal_conductivity!(workspace, data, P, T, X, cp_R)\n\nReturn Dixon–Lewis thermal conductivity [W/(m K)] without computing the multicomponent diffusion matrix. This updates thermal diffusion coefficients and the thermal workspace; workspace.diffusion retains its previous value. Input requirements are the same as for multicomponent_transport!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.multicomponent_thermal_conductivity!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.multicomponent_transport!-Tuple{MultiTransportWorkspace, MultiTransportData, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"API","text":"multicomponent_transport!(workspace, data, P, T, X, cp_R)\n\nCompute the Dixon–Lewis multicomponent matrix [m²/s], thermal diffusion coefficients [kg/(m s)], and conductivity [W/(m K)]. Return conductivity and store the other results in workspace.diffusion and workspace.thermal_diffusion. cp_R contains species reference ideal-gas heat capacities divided by R. Mole fractions must be normalized and nonnegative; zero entries use the same 1e-20 transport floor as Cantera. This floor is not renormalized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.multicomponent_transport!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Arrhenius.network_diagnostics","page":"API","text":"network_diagnostics(network_or_rhs, u, t=0.0)\n\nReturn independent named node properties, device flow/heat rates, total and external mass/energy rates, and net elemental rates. Energy accounting includes reservoir enthalpy flow, reservoir wall heat, and imposed isothermal bath heat. Rates are kg/s, W, and kmol of element/s. Reuse a prepared RHS to avoid repeatedly allocating workspaces when evaluating a trajectory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.network_diagnostics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.network_isoutofdomain-Tuple{ReactorNetwork, Any}","page":"API","text":"Check positive temperature/mass and reject species masses below a relative tolerance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.network_isoutofdomain"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Arrhenius.network_jacobian!","page":"API","text":"Dense second-order finite-difference Jacobian, including all device/state couplings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.network_jacobian!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.network_problem-Tuple{ReactorNetwork, Any}","page":"API","text":"Prepare native callbacks and state for a caller-owned stiff ODE solver.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.network_problem"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.network_rhs-Tuple{ReactorNetwork}","page":"API","text":"Prepare an in-place native network RHS. Each concurrent solve needs its own instance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.network_rhs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.network_state-Tuple{ReactorNetwork}","page":"API","text":"Return independent species-mass/temperature initial states in named-node order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.network_state"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.plasma_properties-Tuple{PlasmaState}","page":"API","text":"Return independent composition arrays and the two-temperature ideal-plasma properties.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.plasma_properties"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.plasma_rates-Tuple{PlasmaState}","page":"API","text":"Return native rate coefficients, progress/production rates and isotropic EEDF at a state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.plasma_rates"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.plasma_thermodynamics-Tuple{PlasmaState}","page":"API","text":"plasma_thermodynamics(state)\n\nReturn partial molar enthalpies (J/kmol), heat capacities (J/kmol/K), reference entropies (J/kmol/K), and mixture mass-specific enthalpy and heat capacity. Heavy species use the gas temperature; electrons use the stored electron temperature. The mixture heat capacity includes each species' heat capacity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.plasma_thermodynamics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.radiation_source-Tuple{CounterflowDiffusionFlame}","page":"API","text":"Return (heat_loss, planck_absorption) at all grid points, in W/m³ and 1/m. Positive heat loss cools the gas. Values are zero when radiation is disabled; the last inlet heat-loss entry is zero, matching Cantera's diagnostic convention.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.radiation_source"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.rate_constant-Tuple{BlowersMaselRate, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"Evaluate a Blowers–Masel forward rate constant from temperature and reaction enthalpy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.rate_constant"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.reaction_rate_constants-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution}","page":"API","text":"Forward, reverse and concentration-equilibrium constants at an ideal-gas state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.reaction_rate_constants"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Arrhenius.reactor_jacobian!","page":"API","text":"reactor_jacobian!(J, u, rhs, t=0)\n\nEvaluate a dense finite-difference Jacobian of a prepared ReactorRHS. Second-order central differences are used inside the physical domain and second-order forward differences at zero mass fractions. Negative trial mass fractions use second-order backward differences, sampling the RHS at u[j] - step and u[j] - 2 * step so no positive perturbation crosses the clipped concentration branch of the RHS. This callback avoids passing automatic-differentiation scalars through the fixed-type workspace. The input state is unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.reactor_jacobian!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.reactor_problem-Tuple{IdealGasReactor, Any}","page":"API","text":"reactor_problem(reactor, tspan)\n\nPrepare a solver-independent named tuple (f, jac, tgrad, u0, tspan, p). The callbacks use the SciML in-place calling convention. tgrad is zero for this autonomous system. Construct a solver's ODE problem from these fields. Each call allocates independent workspaces for safe concurrent integrations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.reactor_problem"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Arrhenius.reactor_properties","page":"API","text":"reactor_properties(reactor, u=reactor_state(reactor))\n\nReturn temperature (K), pressure (Pa), density (kg/m³), composition, specific heat capacities (J/kg/K), enthalpy and internal energy (J/kg), mass-fraction sum, and elemental inventories (kmol of element/kg). Values use the unprojected state, so mass, element, and energy drift can be checked after integration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.reactor_properties"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.reactor_rhs-Tuple{IdealGasReactor}","page":"API","text":"reactor_rhs(reactor)\n\nPrepare a callable rhs!(du, u, p, t) using native Arrhenius kinetics and thermodynamics. p is unused. The workspace has the same scalar type as the initial state; use reactor_jacobian! with solvers requiring derivatives.\n\nNegative trial mass fractions are clipped only when evaluating reaction concentrations. The state and its mass/element balances are never projected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.reactor_rhs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.reactor_state-Tuple{IdealGasReactor}","page":"API","text":"Return an independent initial state vector [mass_fractions; temperature].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.reactor_state"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.read_eedf_model-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"read_eedf_model(path; phase=nothing, data_paths=String[]) -> EEDFModel\n\nRead electron-collision cross sections from a Cantera-style plasma YAML file. Only Boltzmann-two-term EEDF definitions are supported. Collision reaction sections are resolved from the selected phase, including <file>/reactions imports searched through data_paths. Root-level electron-collisions entries require an explicit kind. Reaction kinds use an explicit kind when present; otherwise they are inferred from selected species composition and product charge. For reaction entries, zero thresholds are inferred from the first positive tabulated energy only for excitation, ionization, and attachment collisions. The selector-free electron-collisions layout keeps its legacy Electron identity and explicit root thresholds without resolving thermodynamic imports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.read_eedf_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.read_flame_csv-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"read_flame_csv(path) -> (; columns, data)\n\nRead a flame CSV into tabulated data. Both layouts are recognized: the native save_flame table (z_m,velocity_m_s,temperature_K,density_kg_m3,...) and the equivalent Cantera table (grid,velocity,T,D,X_<species>...). columns holds the header entries and data is a Matrix{Float64} with one row per saved grid point, in file order. Rows or columns may be pruned before the table is passed to set_initial_guess!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.read_flame_csv"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.redlich_kwong_properties!-Tuple{RedlichKwongWorkspace, RedlichKwongThermo, Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"redlich_kwong_properties!(work, model, T, rho, X)\n\nEvaluate a homogeneous RK state from kelvin, kg/m³ and normalized mole fractions. The returned h/u/s/cp/cv are J/kmol or J/kmol/K; hmass etc. use kg. `uTV[k] = ∂U/∂n[k]|T,V,n[j≠k]` is the energy coefficient for reacting constant-volume systems. It differs from ordinary partial molar internal energy. Returned vector properties alias workspace storage until its next evaluation. Only mechanically stable states with positive pressure and heat capacity are accepted. Species polynomials are evaluated at the supplied temperature without clamping, as in Cantera. Users must check their fit ranges and the accuracy of the cubic EOS for their application.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.redlich_kwong_properties!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.redlich_kwong_rates!-Tuple{Any, Arrhenius.Solution, RedlichKwongThermo, Any, Any, Any, RealGasKineticsWorkspace}","page":"API","text":"redlich_kwong_rates!(output, gas, model, T, rho, X, work; rate_multipliers=nothing)\n\nEvaluate species production in kmol/m³/s. X is normalized mole composition. Third-body and falloff rates use physical concentrations. Reaction mass action uses fugacity-based activity concentrations, and PLOG uses the EOS pressure. Equilibrium constants use ideal reference species h/s at one_atm; the phase standard-pressure and standard-concentration terms cancel to this reference. Returns the thermodynamic state, with arrays aliasing work. Supported reaction models are elementary Arrhenius, third-body, Lindemann, Troe and PLOG. Blowers–Masel reactions are explicitly rejected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.redlich_kwong_rates!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.redlich_kwong_state-Tuple{RedlichKwongThermo}","page":"API","text":"redlich_kwong_state(model; T, X, P=nothing, rho=nothing, basis=:mole, root=:gas)\n\nReturn native RK mixture and partial properties from T/P or T/density. The largest physical cubic root is selected by default; root=:liquid explicitly selects the smallest. Selection does not perform phase-equilibrium stability or flash calculations. All arguments use SI units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.redlich_kwong_state"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.restore_flame!-Tuple{Arrhenius.AbstractPremixedFlame, AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"restore_flame!(flame, path)\n\nRestore a native .npz snapshot into a flame using the same mechanism and kind. For multicomponent or Soret snapshots, supply matching MultiTransportData on the destination flame. A restored state is marked converged only if its current native steady residual satisfies the solver tolerance. Snapshots fingerprint the numerical mechanism representation and are intended for the same Julia version. Older archives without a profile-grid policy restore :full_knots; missing spatial discretization retains historical finite-difference behavior. Invalid archives leave the destination unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.restore_flame!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.save_flame-Tuple{AbstractString, Arrhenius.AbstractPremixedFlame}","page":"API","text":"save_flame(path, flame; overwrite=false, basis=:mass)\n\nSave a .npz restart snapshot or a .csv table. Snapshots retain the grid, boundary conditions, temperature profile, transport options, spatial discretization, profile-grid policy, and native state. CSV columns contain position [m], velocity [m/s], temperature [K], density [kg/m³], heat release [W/m³] and species fractions (basis=:mass or :mole).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.save_flame"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.set_catalytic_inlet!-Tuple{CatalyticImpingingJet, Any}","page":"API","text":"Change the inlet composition while retaining the current catalytic solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.set_catalytic_inlet!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.set_catalytic_reactions!-Tuple{CatalyticImpingingJet}","page":"API","text":"Change reaction multipliers and coverage equation mode, retaining the flow profile.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.set_catalytic_reactions!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.set_electric_field!-Tuple{IonizedFlame, Bool}","page":"API","text":"Enable or freeze charged diffusion and the electric-field equation for the next solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.set_electric_field!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.set_equivalence_ratio-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Any}","page":"API","text":"set_equivalence_ratio(gas, phi; fuel, oxidizer, basis=:mole,\n                      diluent=nothing, fraction=nothing)\n\nReturn mole fractions at the requested equivalence ratio. Complete oxidation forms CO2, H2O and SO2; other elements are inert in the mixture definition. Optional dilution specifies exactly one :diluent, :fuel, or :oxidizer fraction in a dictionary or named tuple, using the supplied basis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.set_equivalence_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.set_initial_guess!-Tuple{Arrhenius.AbstractPremixedFlame, AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"set_initial_guess!(flame, data)\n\nLoad a restart initial guess from tabulated flame data, following Cantera's FlameBase.set_initial_guess(data=...). data is a CSV path (see read_flame_csv) or a (; columns, data) table, for example a pruned subset of a previous save_flame export. The flame adopts the data grid and takes its temperature, velocity and species fractions from the table; the temperature profile is shifted so its inlet matches the flame inlet, species absent from the table start at zero (renormalizing the supplied fractions per grid point, as Cantera's SolutionArray restore does; a table without any species columns keeps the inlet composition), and boundary conditions are not overwritten. For a FreeFlame the saved velocity becomes the mass-flux profile, and the fixed-temperature point is reselected on the loaded profile; for a BurnerFlame the prescribed mass flux is retained. The flame is marked unconverged; call solve! to continue from the guess.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.set_initial_guess!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.set_mass_flux!-Tuple{Arrhenius.AbstractPremixedCounterflow}","page":"API","text":"Change positive inlet mass-flux magnitudes [kg/(m² s)] while retaining the current profile.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.set_mass_flux!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.set_mean_electron_energy!-Tuple{PlasmaState, Any}","page":"API","text":"Change electron energy in eV, retaining gas temperature, composition and density.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.set_mean_electron_energy!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.set_mixture_fraction-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Any}","page":"API","text":"set_mixture_fraction(gas, Z; fuel, oxidizer, basis=:mole)\n\nReturn mole fractions with fuel mass fraction Z in the combined streams. basis describes the two input stream compositions, not Z.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.set_mixture_fraction"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.set_plasma_enthalpy!-Tuple{PlasmaState, Any}","page":"API","text":"set_plasma_enthalpy!(state, enthalpy; pressure, rtol=1e-12, maxiter=500)\n\nSet mass-specific enthalpy in J/kg and pressure in Pa by solving for gas temperature. Retain composition, electron energy, electric field, EEDF and mobility. A failed inversion leaves the state unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.set_plasma_enthalpy!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.set_plasma_state!-Tuple{PlasmaState}","page":"API","text":"set_plasma_state!(state; temperature, pressure, mole_fractions, mass_fractions)\n\nSet gas temperature, pressure and optionally composition while retaining electron energy. The density follows the two-temperature equation of state. With no composition argument the current mass fractions are retained.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.set_plasma_state!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.set_porous_medium!-Tuple{DustyGasTransport}","page":"API","text":"Update medium properties and invalidate cached coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.set_porous_medium!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Arrhenius.set_radiation!","page":"API","text":"set_radiation!(flame, enabled=true; boundary_emissivities=(0,0))\n\nEnable or disable the gray optically thin H2O/CO2 radiation model. Emissivities of the fuel and oxidizer boundaries must lie in [0,1]. The current solution is retained for continuation; call solve!(flame; refine_grid=false) to repeat the official nonradiating-to-radiating calculation on the same grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.set_radiation!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.set_reduced_electric_field!-Tuple{PlasmaState, Any}","page":"API","text":"set_reduced_electric_field!(state, reduced_field)\n\nSet E/N in V·m² using the current total number density. The absolute electric field is retained across subsequent gas-state changes. This does not update the electron distribution or its cached mobility; call update_eedf! afterward.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.set_reduced_electric_field!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.set_states-Tuple{Arrhenius.Solution, Any, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"\"mainly for testing code, will be removed in the future\" \"please customize such functions following this example\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.set_states"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.set_temperature_profile!-Tuple{BurnerFlame, Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"set_temperature_profile!(flame::BurnerFlame, positions, temperatures;\n                         relative=true, grid_policy=:full_knots)\n\nPrescribe temperature [K] using piecewise-linear interpolation. Relative positions span 0–1; absolute positions are in meters. The profile must cover the domain and match the burner temperature at its inlet. All supplied pairs are copied and retained when the solution grid changes.\n\nFor conservative flames, grid_policy=:full_knots inserts every interior profile knot. :adaptive retains the current grid and adds cells during solve! wherever the exact profile-to-cell-chord error exceeds 1% of the largest supplied temperature, in addition to species and spacing refinement. Adaptive profiles require conservative discretization and no Soret diffusion. Enabling Soret later restores full knots. Finite-difference flames retain their historical profile sampling behavior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.set_temperature_profile!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.set_transport!-Tuple{Arrhenius.AbstractPremixedFlame, Any}","page":"API","text":"set_transport!(flame, model; data=flame.multicomponent_data, soret=false)\n\nSelect :mixture_averaged or :multicomponent diffusion. Multicomponent diffusion and either model's Soret diffusion require MultiTransportData exported for the same mechanism. soret=true includes thermal diffusion. Use flux_gradient_basis=:mass or :mole for mixture-averaged diffusion. An existing solution is retained as the initial guess for the next solve!. Enabling Soret on an adaptive prescribed-temperature grid first inserts all profile knots and interpolates the state, changing its policy to :full_knots. Invalid transport arguments leave the flame unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.set_transport!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.set_two_point_control!-Tuple{CounterflowDiffusionFlame}","page":"API","text":"set_two_point_control!(flame; temperature, decrement=0)\n\nEnable two-point temperature control: fixes the temperature [K] at the interior grid points nearest the first (fuel side) and last (oxidizer side) crossing of temperature, recording the actual selected grid temperatures minus decrement [K]. A positive oxidizer mass-flux auxiliary row [kg/(m² s)] is inserted before the spread-rate row on first enable; the inlet mass fluxes become unknowns. temperature must lie in 200–6000 K and exceed both inlet temperatures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.set_two_point_control!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.solve!-Tuple{Arrhenius.AbstractPremixedCounterflow}","page":"API","text":"Solve the axisymmetric flow with native equilibrium initialization and adaptive refinement. Time-step controls are as for premixed flames. The default refinement_interpolation=:velocity interpolates axial velocity and reconstructs mass flux with the residual density function; :mass_flux retains linear interpolation of the stored mass-flux component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.solve!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.solve!-Tuple{Arrhenius.AbstractPremixedFlame}","page":"API","text":"solve!(flame; refine_grid=true, ratio=3, slope=0.06, curve=0.12, prune=0,\n       max_points=1000, max_time_steps=500, loglevel=0, auto=false)\n\nSolve a native premixed flame using damped Newton iterations and implicit pseudo-time stepping. Failure raises an error and leaves converged=false. auto=true mirrors Cantera's FreeFlame.solve(auto=True) for FreeFlame: a staged ladder on uniform 12/24/48-point grids from fresh analytic guesses with Soret disabled, one refinement-enabled solve, then a final stage with Soret restored — all wrapped in the domain-expansion loop that doubles a too-narrow domain and restarts the ladder. Cantera's time-step controls: time_step_growth_factor, time_step_growth_strategy (:fixed_growth, :steady_norm, :transient_residual, :residual_ratio, :newton_iterations), time_step_callback(dt), time_step_regrid and jacobian_mode (:auto or :finite_difference). prune is Cantera's fourth refine criterion: grid points whose slope/curve indicators fall below it are removed on each refinement pass (0 disables pruning; it must not exceed slope or curve).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.solve!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.solve!-Tuple{CatalyticImpingingJet}","page":"API","text":"Solve coupled native gas and surface equations, with adaptive axial refinement.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.solve!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.solve!-Tuple{CounterflowDiffusionFlame}","page":"API","text":"Solve the native axisymmetric counterflow diffusion flame. With auto=true, recover failed or extinguished coarse-grid solves using the native prescribed initial temperature profile, then progressively finer initial grids. Failure to find a burning solution raises an error and leaves converged=false. With auto=false, a converged extinguished state is returned for extinction continuation. Two-point control always retains the current branch and disables automatic reinitialization; call set_two_point_control! to choose new targets. Time-step controls (time_step_growth_factor, time_step_growth_strategy, time_step_callback, time_step_regrid, jacobian_mode) are as for premixed flames.\n\nDiffusion solves use the same success-counted steady retries and dependent-row transient skip as the other counterflow kinds, matching Cantera's algebraic-row handling. The Nakamura diffusion case is substantially more expensive with this correct handling; performance work is tracked separately. See audit.md and verify.md for source references and measured validation. :residual_ratio growth is supported for this diffusion kind only among the counterflow kinds; premixed, twin-premixed, and stagnation require Cantera boundary-residual parity before using it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.solve!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.solve_eedf-Tuple{EEDFModel, EEDFState}","page":"API","text":"solve_eedf(model, state; options=TwoTermOptions(), initial=nothing)\n\nSolve the fixed-grid temporal two-term EEDF problem. Molecular weights in state are kg/kmol and reduced_field is in V*m^2. Pass a previous EEDFResult as initial to continue from its center distribution on the same grid. The input is copied. At or below options.low_field_threshold, the calculation always starts from the gas-temperature Maxwellian.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.solve_eedf"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.solve_network-Tuple{ReactorNetwork, Any}","page":"API","text":"Integrate with integrator(problem; kwargs...), using the same contract as solve_reactor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.solve_network"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.solve_network_steady-Tuple{ReactorNetwork}","page":"API","text":"solve_network_steady(network; integrator, interval=1, max_time=100,\n                     steady_tolerance=1e-8, initial_state=network_state(network), kwargs...)\n\nRelax an autonomous network in finite integration intervals until species-mass rates divided by vessel mass and temperature rates divided by temperature are below steady_tolerance (s⁻¹). The caller's solution must expose its states as solution.u. Returns (state, time, residual); throws if convergence is not reached by max_time. Time-varying boundary conditions require transient solves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.solve_network_steady"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.solve_reactor-Tuple{IdealGasReactor, Any}","page":"API","text":"solve_reactor(reactor, tspan; integrator, kwargs...)\n\nIntegrate with a caller-supplied integrator(problem; kwargs...), where problem is the named tuple from reactor_problem. Returns the integrator's solution unchanged. The core package has no mandatory ODE solver dependency.\n\nFor example, after loading SciMLBase and OrdinaryDiffEqBDF:\n\nfunction bdf(problem; kwargs...)\n    f = ODEFunction(problem.f; jac=problem.jac, tgrad=problem.tgrad)\n    ode = ODEProblem(f, problem.u0, problem.tspan, problem.p)\n    solve(ode, QNDF(); kwargs...)\nend\nsolution = solve_reactor(reactor, (0.0, 0.001); integrator=bdf,\n                         reltol=1e-8, abstol=1e-14, saveat=1e-5)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.solve_reactor"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.solve_surface-Tuple{IdealSurface, Any}","page":"API","text":"Integrate surface coverages with a caller-owned stiff ODE integrator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.solve_surface"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.solve_surface_flow-Tuple{SurfaceFlowReactor, Any}","page":"API","text":"Solve the stationary surface DAE with caller-owned native stiff integrators.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.solve_surface_flow"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.solver_stats-Tuple{Arrhenius.AbstractPremixedFlame}","page":"API","text":"solver_stats(flame) -> stats\n\nReturn the mutable solve-counter object owned by flame, mirroring Cantera's per-instance Sim1D.solver_stats: a PremixedStats for FreeFlame/ BurnerFlame (and ionized premixed flames), a CounterflowStats for the counterflow and impinging-jet kinds. These counters are a diagnostic only; they never influence solver decisions.\n\nCounted fields per kind (a field a kind never increments stays zero):\n\npremixed kinds (FreeFlame, BurnerFlame, IonizedFlame): newton_calls, newton_iterations, jacobians, residual_evals, time_steps, time_step_failures, steady_failures, regrids, grids;\ncounterflow kinds (CounterflowDiffusionFlame, the premixed-counterflow wrappers, impinging and catalytic jets): all of the above plus fd_jacobians, chem_jac_failures, recoveries and per-attempt calls tuples — except catalytic jets, which record grids but never calls.\n\nresidual_evals counts every call to the public residual evaluator (flame_residual!/counterflow_residual!) at its entry, before argument validation: damped-step trials, time-step-growth probes and finite-difference Jacobian columns are all included. Cantera's counterpart excludes the finite-difference Jacobian evaluations (OneDim::eval counts only count!=0 calls, and evalJacobian passes 0), so native residual_evals runs higher than Cantera's on finite-difference solves.\n\nExact semantics:\n\nsolve! resets the flame's counters at entry and they then report that solve! only. The reset happens after the keyword-argument validation, so an invalid ratio/slope/prune/... argument throws with the previous counts intact. Checks that run after the reset point throw with the counters already zeroed (counterflow two-point-control validation, catalytic transport requirements) or partially counted (runtime errors such as a max_points overrun or auto non-convergence).\nDirect calls to the internal helpers (_flame_newton!, _flame_steady!, _refine_flame!, _counterflow_newton!, _counterflow_steady!) accumulate on top of the current counters without resetting them.\nA steady attempt that throws is not rolled back: the scalar counters it already incremented stay, and its grids entry (pushed at attempt entry) stays, but counterflow calls summaries are appended only when the attempt returns, so a thrown attempt gets none.\nImmutable objects driven through the helpers (property adapters, test fixtures) count into the family global instead of a per-flame entry.\nThe exported PREMIXED_STATS/COUNTERFLOW_STATS globals mirror the accumulated family totals across all flames and are never reset by solve!; call Arrhenius.reset! on them manually for per-run attribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.solver_stats"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.solver_stats-Tuple{CounterflowDiffusionFlame}","page":"API","text":"solver_stats(f::CounterflowDiffusionFlame) -> CounterflowStats\n\nPer-flame solve counters; see Arrhenius.solver_stats for the exact reset, accumulation and failure semantics. Beyond the shared counters the counterflow kinds also count fd_jacobians (chemistry-Jacobian fallbacks to finite differences), chem_jac_failures and recoveries, and append one calls tuple per _counterflow_steady! attempt — (grid, dt, newton_iterations, jacobians, time_steps, time_step_failures, wall_seconds, converged) — when that attempt returns; attempts that throw get their grids entry only (catalytic jets record grids but never calls). All counterflow kinds count residual_evals once per counterflow_residual! call, including the wrapper and catalytic methods, which delegate here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.solver_stats"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.species_index-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API","text":"Get species index of a species\n\nExample\n\n    species_index(gas, \"O2\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.species_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.species_thermo-Tuple{IdealGasThermo, Any}","page":"API","text":"species_thermo(thermo::IdealGasThermo, T; P=one_atm)\n\nEvaluate dimensionless ideal-gas standard-state (cp_R, h_RT, s_R) for every species, in the phase's declared order. T is kelvin and P is pascals. NASA7, multi-region NASA9, Shomate, and constant-cp models may coexist. This standalone evaluation needs no reaction or transport sidecar. For example, species_thermo(IdealGasThermo(YAML.load_file(\"airNASA9.yaml\")), 8000.0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.species_thermo"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.steady_coverages-Tuple{IdealSurface}","page":"API","text":"steady_coverages(surface; integrator, interval=1, max_time=1e5,\n                 steady_tolerance=1e-6, kwargs...)\n\nIntegrate fixed-gas coverage equations until the maximum absolute coverage derivative is below steady_tolerance in s⁻¹. Returns the coverages, residual, elapsed physical time, and final integration solution. Temperatures and pressure are exactly those supplied to IdealSurface throughout the solve. At a restart, negative roundoff coverages within 1e-13 are set to zero and the site fractions are normalized before continuing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.steady_coverages"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.surface_flow_diagnostics-Tuple{SurfaceFlowReactor, Any}","page":"API","text":"Mass, momentum, elemental fluxes, and stationary-surface residual at one axial state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.surface_flow_diagnostics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.surface_flow_problem-Tuple{SurfaceFlowReactor, Any}","page":"API","text":"surface_flow_problem(flow, length; initial_coverages)\n\nReturn a constant-mass-matrix DAE in axial distance. The first gas-species rows are differential and the remaining coverage rows are algebraic. Supply stationary inlet coverages, or use solve_surface_flow to initialize them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.surface_flow_problem"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.surface_flow_properties-Tuple{SurfaceFlowReactor, Any}","page":"API","text":"Recover the subsonic pressure, density, and speed from conserved momentum and mass flux.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.surface_flow_properties"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Arrhenius.surface_jacobian!","page":"API","text":"Second-order finite-difference coverage Jacobian, including rate dependencies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.surface_jacobian!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Arrhenius.surface_rates","page":"API","text":"Calculate an independent copy of gas/surface/bulk production and coverage rates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.surface_rates"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.surface_rates!-Tuple{SurfaceWorkspace, SurfaceMechanism, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"API","text":"surface_rates!(workspace, mechanism, T, P, X, coverages; gas_temperature=T)\nsurface_rates!(workspace, surface, coverages=surface.coverages)\n\nCalculate elementary reaction and species production rates per unit surface area. workspace.production_rates is in kmol/m²/s in mechanism species order; coverage_rates is in s⁻¹ and includes each species' site size. X and coverages must already be normalized. Tiny negative trial coverages are clipped only while evaluating rates; accepted ODE states are domain checked.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.surface_rates!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.surface_reactor_chain-Tuple{SurfaceFlowReactor, Any}","page":"API","text":"surface_reactor_chain(flow, length; reactors=201, porosity=0.3,\n                     integrator, kwargs...)\n\nMarch a chain of isothermal stirred reactors to steady state. Each volume is area*length/(reactors-1)*porosity; its surface area is surface_area_per_length*length/(reactors-1). As in surf_pfr_chain.py, the first reported state is the first reactor outlet at coordinate zero. Returns all gas mole/mass fractions, coverages, pressures, and dimensionless steady residuals. A damped Newton solve enforces fixed-pressure species and site balances; native stiff integration supplies a stable fallback initial guess.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.surface_reactor_chain"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.temperature-Tuple{Arrhenius.AbstractPremixedFlame}","page":"API","text":"Temperature [K] at each grid point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.temperature"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.twin_flame_diagnostics-Tuple{CounterflowTwinPremixedFlame}","page":"API","text":"Return consumption speed [m/s], characteristic upstream strain rate [1/s], and its grid index for a twin flame. Definitions follow the published Cantera twin flame example: integrate heat release divided by mass heat capacity, and use the largest forward velocity gradient upstream of the preheat velocity minimum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.twin_flame_diagnostics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.update_eedf!-Tuple{PlasmaEnergyRHS, Any}","page":"API","text":"Refresh the accepted-state EEDF and cached collision rate coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.update_eedf!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.update_eedf!-Tuple{PlasmaState}","page":"API","text":"update_eedf!(state; options=TwoTermOptions())\n\nSolve the Boltzmann distribution at the current gas state and electric field, continuing from the previous distribution when appropriate. Cache the result and mobility without changing the independently stored electron temperature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.update_eedf!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.velocity-Tuple{Arrhenius.AbstractPremixedFlame}","page":"API","text":"Axial velocity [m/s] at each grid point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.velocity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.water_rankine-Tuple{PureWater}","page":"API","text":"water_rankine(model; inlet_temperature=300, boiler_pressure=8e5,\n               pump_efficiency=0.6, turbine_efficiency=0.8)\n\nCompute a Rankine cycle starting with saturated liquid, with an adiabatic pump, constant-pressure heating to saturated vapor, and an adiabatic turbine. Returns the four states, the two isentropic comparison states, work and heat in J/kg, and thermal efficiency. Inputs and outputs use SI units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.water_rankine"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.water_saturation-Tuple{PureWater, Any}","page":"API","text":"Return saturation pressure [Pa] and saturated liquid/vapor densities [kg/m³].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.water_saturation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.water_state-Tuple{PureWater}","page":"API","text":"water_state(model::PureWater; T, P)\nwater_state(model::PureWater; T, Q)\nwater_state(model::PureWater; P, Q)\nwater_state(model::PureWater; h, P)\nwater_state(model::PureWater; s, P)\n\nConstruct an equilibrium liquid/vapor water state from exactly two independent SI properties. Q is vapor mass fraction, h specific enthalpy and s specific entropy. (T,v) and (u,P) are also supported. The EOS excludes ice and uses the temperature limits of the Reynolds model; TP on saturation is ambiguous. State fields include T,P,Q,rho,v,h,u,s,cp,cv,g,a. Two-phase cp is infinite and cv is NaN, matching the convention used by Cantera's Reynolds water backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.water_state"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.wdot!-NTuple{7, Any}","page":"API","text":"Compute reaction source terms into preallocated storage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.wdot!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Arrhenius.wdot_func-NTuple{5, Any}","page":"API","text":"compute reaction source term dC/dt\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Arrhenius.wdot_func"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#About-the-package","page":"About the package","text":"Arrhenius.jl is a Julia package for reacting-flow and combustion modeling, including differentiable chemistry workflows.","title":"About the package"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Publications","page":"About the package","text":"Arrhenius.jl: A Differentiable Combustion Simulation Package\nMachine Learning Approaches to Learn HyChem Models\nNeural Differential Equations for Inverse Modeling in Model Combustors\nSGD-based Optimization in Modeling Combustion Kinetics","title":"Publications"},{"category":"section","location":"concepts/#Related-projects","page":"About the package","text":"ArrheniusActiveSubspace: sensitivity analysis and uncertainty quantification.\nArrheniusFlame1D: one-dimensional flame sensitivity analysis.\nCRNN_HyChem: neural-network approaches to learning kinetic mechanisms.\nDeepReduction: mechanism reduction workflows.\n\nRelated software includes Cantera for mechanism processing and reference calculations and ReactionMechanismSimulator.jl for chemical mechanism simulation.\n\nFor the package license, see the MIT License.","title":"Related projects"},{"category":"section","location":"mechanisms/#Mechanisms-and-preprocessing","page":"Mechanisms and preprocessing","text":"CreateSolution(\"mechanism.yaml\") loads species thermodynamics, reaction data, and transport fits from the YAML file and its .yaml.npz sidecar. The mechanism exporter uses Cantera's YAML parser during preprocessing; Cantera is not required when running Julia calculations.\n\nThe sidecar exporter supports elementary and three-body Arrhenius reactions, Lindemann and Troe falloff, pressure-dependent Arrhenius (PLOG) rates, Blowers–Masel rates, explicit reaction orders, and supported NASA7, NASA9, Shomate, and constant-cp thermodynamic models. Unsupported rate models are rejected during export.\n\nYAML phase definitions may select local or imported species and reaction sections. Imported files are resolved relative to the mechanism file; optional data_paths can provide additional search directories:\n\ngas = CreateSolution(\"mechanism.yaml\"; data_paths=[\"shared-data\"])\n\nRegenerate the sidecar after changing the mechanism or any imported YAML file. For multicomponent diffusion or Soret diffusion, export matching collision-integral data with mechanism/export_multicomponent.py; see flame transport.","title":"Mechanisms and preprocessing"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Examples","page":"Examples","text":"The repository examples are executable entry points. Some require prepared mechanism sidecars or an optional Julia environment; each script describes its inputs near the top. Run commands from the repository root unless noted.","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Reactors","page":"Examples","text":"Constant-pressure ignition: adiabatic H₂/O₂/Ar ignition with QNDF.\nConstant-volume ignition: closed constant-volume ignition.\nIsothermal reactor: compare constant-pressure and constant-volume reactors.\nReactor networks: flow devices, stream mixing, heat-transfer walls, and pistons.\nEngine: a moving-piston model with prescribed injection and valves.\nPlasma reactors and nanosecond-pulse discharge.","title":"Reactors"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Flames","page":"Examples","text":"Free premixed flame: transport-stage sequence for H₂/O₂/Ar.\nBurner-stabilized flame: prescribed inlet mass flux.\nFixed-temperature burner flame: measured temperature profile.\nCounterflow diffusion, opposed premixed, and twin premixed.\nInert-wall impinging flame and catalytic impinging flame.\nInitial guess and restart workflow and ionized flames.","title":"Flames"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/#Thermodynamics,-transport,-and-chemistry","page":"Examples","text":"Thermodynamic calculations: equilibrium, sound speeds, stream mixing, and nozzles.\nGas and condensed-phase equilibrium: gas/graphite equilibrium.\nReal-gas properties: Redlich–Kwong and Helmholtz-based calculations.\nParallel transport sweep: viscosity and thermal conductivity over a temperature range.\nSurface chemistry and catalytic flow: surface kinetics, catalytic reactors, and diamond growth.\nElectron-energy distribution and Blowers–Masel rates.\nMechanism preprocessing: exporters for kinetics, transport, surface, condensed-phase, and real-gas data.","title":"Thermodynamics, transport, and chemistry"},{"category":"section","location":"reactors/#Zero-dimensional-reactors","page":"Zero-dimensional reactors","text":"IdealGasReactor defines a homogeneous closed reactor at constant pressure or volume, with adiabatic or isothermal energy treatment. It provides the state equations and Jacobian independently of any ODE package. Pass the problem to an integrator through solve_reactor.","title":"Zero-dimensional reactors"},{"category":"section","location":"reactors/#Constant-pressure-ignition","page":"Zero-dimensional reactors","text":"From the repository root, activate example/reactors and run this example. The optional environment includes SciMLBase and OrdinaryDiffEqBDF; the helper selects QNDF and connects the Arrhenius Jacobian to the integrator.\n\nusing Arrhenius\ninclude(\"example/reactors/ode_solver.jl\")\n\ngas = CreateSolution(\"mechanism/h2o2.yaml\")\nreactor = IdealGasReactor(gas; temperature=1001.0, pressure=one_atm,\n    mole_fractions=Dict(\"H2\" => 2, \"O2\" => 1, \"AR\" => 4))\nsolution = solve_reactor(reactor, (0.0, 0.001); integrator=native_bdf,\n    reltol=1e-8, abstol=1e-14)\nfinal = reactor_properties(reactor, solution.u[end])\nprintln((temperature_K=final.temperature, pressure_Pa=final.pressure))\n\nreactor_properties reports thermodynamic state and conserved quantities so callers can inspect integration drift. Arrhenius.jl does not select a mandatory integrator. See the reactor examples.","title":"Constant-pressure ignition"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Validation-methods","page":"Validation methods","text":"Arrhenius.jl validation uses two distinct comparisons.","title":"Validation methods"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Same-discrete-problem-comparison","page":"Validation methods","text":"To compare solver implementations, both evaluate the same discrete problem: the same grid coordinates, physical state, boundary conditions, mechanism, transport model, tolerances, and solver controls. Residuals are compared in physical units at that shared state. This checks implementation parity for a declared configuration; it does not establish accuracy on a different grid.","title":"Same-discrete-problem comparison"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Accuracy-against-a-converged-reference","page":"Validation methods","text":"To assess solution accuracy, a flame solution is compared with a separately converged reference. Grid-refinement studies estimate how the result changes as spatial resolution increases. Solver convergence and grid accuracy are checked separately; a small change between two grids alone is not treated as proof of convergence.\n\nValidation cases must represent burning flames when the question concerns a burning solution. Extinguished solutions are reported as extinguished and are not counted as successful burning cases.\n\nThese methods describe the validation approach. They do not assert a particular accuracy level for every mechanism, flame family, or operating condition.","title":"Accuracy against a converged reference"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#Installation","page":"Installation","text":"Arrhenius.jl requires Julia. Install the package with Julia's package manager:\n\n]\nadd https://github.com/DENG-MIT/Arrhenius.jl\n\nA local checkout can be developed with Pkg.develop(path=\".\"). The optional reactor example environment contains SciML solver dependencies used by the zero-dimensional examples.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#Mechanism-files","page":"Installation","text":"CreateSolution reads a Cantera YAML mechanism and a same-name .yaml.npz sidecar. The repository includes prepared sidecars for its example mechanisms. For another mechanism, install Cantera and NumPy, then run:\n\npython mechanism/export_sidecar.py path/to/mechanism.yaml\n\nFor multicomponent or Soret transport, also create a .multicomponent.npz file:\n\npython mechanism/export_multicomponent.py path/to/mechanism.yaml path/to/mechanism.yaml.multicomponent.npz\n\nRegenerate sidecars whenever the YAML or an imported file changes. See mechanism support for supported rate and thermodynamic models.","title":"Mechanism files"},{"category":"section","location":"#Arrhenius.jl","page":"Arrhenius.jl","text":"Arrhenius.jl provides Julia-native tools for chemical kinetics and reacting-flow calculations. It reads Cantera YAML mechanisms and uses preprocessed sidecar files for kinetic and transport data. Cantera is not required at calculation time.","title":"Arrhenius.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Start-here","page":"Arrhenius.jl","text":"Installation explains how to add the package and prepare a mechanism.\nMechanisms describes supported mechanism data and sidecars.\nZero-dimensional reactors introduces ignition calculations and ODE solver integration.\nOne-dimensional flames covers flame types, transport, and discretization.\nSolver options documents refinement, time stepping, and transport controls.\nExamples indexes runnable examples by application.\nValidation methods explains code-parity and solution-accuracy comparisons.\nAPI reference lists exported types and functions.","title":"Start here"},{"category":"section","location":"#Capability-overview","page":"Arrhenius.jl","text":"The package includes ideal-gas thermochemistry, kinetics, equilibrium and sound-speed calculations; constant-pressure and constant-volume reactors; reactor networks; planar and opposed-flow flames; catalytic and ionized models; transport, plasma, porous-media, pure-water, and real-gas tools. See the examples index for entry points.","title":"Capability overview"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citation","page":"Arrhenius.jl","text":"Ji, W. et al., “Arrhenius.jl: A Differentiable Combustion Simulation Package”.","title":"Citation"},{"category":"section","location":"solver_options/#Solver-options","page":"Solver options","text":"","title":"Solver options"},{"category":"section","location":"solver_options/#Spatial-discretization","page":"Solver options","text":"FreeFlame and BurnerFlame accept :finitedifference and :conservative. The constructor default is :finitedifference. Set discretization=:conservative to use finite-volume species and total-enthalpy balances. Ionized flames also accept both options; their initial conservative solve first uses a frozen finite-difference stage.","title":"Spatial discretization"},{"category":"section","location":"solver_options/#Grid-refinement-and-solve-controls","page":"Solver options","text":"solve! adaptively refines a grid by default. Common controls are:\n\nratio, slope, and curve: refinement thresholds.\nprune: remove points whose indicators fall below the threshold; 0 disables pruning.\nmaxpoints and maxtime_steps: bounds for the solve.\ninitialtimestep, timestepgrowthfactor, and timestepgrowthstrategy: pseudo-time stepping controls.\ntimestepcallback and timestepregrid: observe or recover from stalled time stepping.\njacobianmode=:auto or :finitedifference: use the analytic or numerical Jacobian path where available.\nauto=true: for FreeFlame, use a staged solve and domain expansion; counterflow solvers use their own recovery sequence.\n\nRefinement settings and supported controls vary by flame family. Consult the corresponding constructor and solve! docstrings in the API reference.","title":"Grid refinement and solve controls"},{"category":"section","location":"solver_options/#Transport","page":"Solver options","text":"settransport!(flame, :mixtureaveraged) selects mixture-averaged diffusion. :multicomponent requires MultiTransportData exported from the same mechanism. Set soret=true to include thermal diffusion; this also requires matching multicomponent data. For mixture-averaged transport, fluxgradientbasis=:mole or :mass chooses the gradient basis.\n\nCounterflow diffusion flames can enable optically thin radiation with set_radiation!. Radiation and transport options apply only to flame families that expose them.","title":"Transport"},{"category":"section","location":"flames/#One-dimensional-flames","page":"One-dimensional flames","text":"Arrhenius.jl provides planar premixed flames, axisymmetric counterflow flames, and catalytic impinging flames. Flame objects are built from a Solution and solved with solve!; the API reference contains all constructors and diagnostics.","title":"One-dimensional flames"},{"category":"section","location":"flames/#Free-and-burner-flames","page":"One-dimensional flames","text":"FreeFlame solves a freely propagating planar premixed flame. Its inlet mass flux, and therefore flame speed, is an eigenvalue. BurnerFlame prescribes inlet mass flux. Both use adaptive spatial grids and support mixture-averaged or multicomponent diffusion, with optional Soret diffusion.\n\nBurnerFlame also supports measured temperature profiles through settemperatureprofile!. The default full-knot policy inserts every supplied profile coordinate. On the conservative, non-Soret path, adaptive refinement adds cells where the profile differs from the cellwise linear chord by more than 1% of the largest supplied temperature. Adaptive profiles require conservative discretization without Soret; enabling Soret restores full knots.","title":"Free and burner flames"},{"category":"section","location":"flames/#Counterflow-and-impinging-flames","page":"One-dimensional flames","text":"CounterflowDiffusionFlame models an axisymmetric fuel/oxidizer diffusion flame. Optional radiation models optically thin CO₂/H₂O loss; two-point control supports continuation on a selected branch.\nCounterflowPremixedFlame models opposed premixed streams.\nCounterflowTwinPremixedFlame models a symmetric twin flame from two premixed streams.\nImpingingJet models an axisymmetric premixed jet directed at an inert wall.\n\nSee the examples index for runnable entry points and required input data.","title":"Counterflow and impinging flames"},{"category":"section","location":"flames/#Ionized-flames","page":"One-dimensional flames","text":"For mechanisms configured with ionized-gas transport, construct an IonizedFlame through FreeFlame or BurnerFlame. Solve first with charged diffusion frozen, then enable the electric field with setelectricfield!(flame, true) and solve again. The model includes charged species and Gauss's law. Soret diffusion, prescribed temperature profiles, and flame restart snapshots are not supported for this type.","title":"Ionized flames"},{"category":"section","location":"flames/#Transport","page":"One-dimensional flames","text":"Multicomponent diffusion and Soret diffusion require collision-integral data exported for the same mechanism. Mixture-averaged diffusion supports mole- or mass-fraction gradients. See solver options for choices and constraints.","title":"Transport"},{"category":"section","location":"flames/#Minimal-flame-calculation","page":"One-dimensional flames","text":"Run from the repository root with the package project activated:\n\nusing Arrhenius\n\ngas = CreateSolution(\"mechanism/h2o2.yaml\")\nflame = FreeFlame(gas; T=300.0, P=one_atm,\n    X=\"H2:1.1,O2:1,AR:5\", width=0.03)\nsolve!(flame)\nprintln(\"Flame speed: \", flame_speed(flame), \" m/s\")","title":"Minimal flame calculation"}]
}
